const reply = await csrfFetch(`${base}/model-calls/reconcile`, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json", "Idempotency-Key": crypto.randomUUID() },
  body: JSON.stringify({ charged_micro_usd: Math.round(dollars * MICRO_USD) }),
}).catch(() => null);
// condensed. On failure: setRefusal(await refusalOf(reply)), and refusalOf(null) says 'Nothing changed.'

When the browser loses the reply (null), the owner sees 'That could not be done. Nothing changed.' When the BFF times out after 30 s, it returns 503 'The local API is unavailable.' even though the API may already have committed. Neither message says the charge may have been recorded, and the amount stays in the field. post_reconcile stores a receipt per key and applies the charge to the oldest uncertain call, so a resubmission under a new key is a new request. If two or more calls are open, the same charge is recorded against a second call. Pause, resume, cancel (in both run-controls and run-panel) and effect reconciliation show the same unproven failure. In UnknownEffects a double press sends two reconciliations, and the second one's refusal ('This was already resolved.') replaces the success note.

