cursor = Math.max(
  cursor,
  (JSON.parse(message.data as string) as { cursor: number }).cursor,
);

If a cursor frame carries no number, cursor becomes NaN, and Math.max keeps it NaN. From then on every replay and reconnect asks for after=NaN, which the API's Cursor parameter (an int with ge=0, event_routes.py:41) rejects, so the page loops. An admission reply without run_id produces a link to /runs/undefined (continue-run.tsx:62, 108-110). Nothing in apps/web uses parseErrorResponse or parseRunAccepted. The stream route is include_in_schema=False (event_routes.py:226), so its frames have no generated parser. ESLint counts 10 type assertions in this area, and grep finds 20 casts of the form `json().catch(() => null)) as` across apps/web. Three of the ten are `message.data as string` on EventSource data, which is always a string; the seven that can hide drift are the cursor frame, the event page, the admission reply and four error bodies.

findings.sketch:
export function parseCursorFrame(data: string): number | null {
  let value: unknown;
  try { value = JSON.parse(data); } catch { return null; }
  if (typeof value !== 'object' || value === null || !('cursor' in value)) return null;
  const cursor = value.cursor;
  return typeof cursor === 'number' && Number.isSafeInteger(cursor) && cursor >= 0 ? cursor : null;
}

